= a
{parent=55}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first transformation is an <Achmatowicz rearrangement>, followed by protection and selective reduction; the last is a <Johnson-Claisen rearrangement>.

* Butyllithium removes the furan 2-proton, giving a 2-lithiofuran. Its carbon attacks the <aldehyde> <carbonyl>; <protonation> of the resulting alkoxide gives the furfuryl alcohol. Carbon-carbon bond formation occurs at the lithiated position.
* Electrophilic oxygen transfer from mCPBA to the electron-rich furan gives an oxidized, ring-opened intermediate. Opening the oxygenated furan gives a hydroxy enedicarbonyl: the original furfuryl hydroxyl then attacks the remote <aldehyde> intramolecularly. This expands the five-membered furan into a six-membered <lactol> containing an enone <carbonyl>. The original furfuryl oxygen becomes the pyran ring oxygen; oxidation of the furan supplies the new <carbonyl> and <lactol> functionality.
* Ag$_2$O/MeI converts the <lactol> OH into its methyl <acetal>: oxygen attacks methyl iodide by <SN2 reaction>, with iodide removal and proton transfer. This protects the anomeric position.
* LiAlH$_4$ delivers hydride to the remaining <ketone> <carbonyl>, followed by <protonation>. The methyl <acetal> survives these basic reducing conditions, leaving the depicted allylic alcohol.
* Under weak-acid catalysis, that allylic OH replaces an ethoxy group of triethyl orthoacetate. Loss of another ethanol molecule, with removal of a methyl proton, gives the mixed <ketene acetal> $\mathrm{CH_2=C(OEt)-O-(allyl)}$. A six-electron, chair-like <Claisen rearrangement> breaks the allyl-O bond, forms a bond between the terminal allyl carbon and the ketene-acetal CH$_2$, and moves the ring double bond one position. <Carbonyl> formation gives the $\mathrm{-CH_2CO_2Et}$ substituent.

The <acetal> protects the other oxygenated position during hydride reduction. The orthoester transfers an acetate-derived two-carbon unit through a <ketene acetal>; it does not directly substitute the allylic OH with an intact acetate ion.

<Simple stereoselective version of the Claisen rearrangement by William S. Johnson and colleagues>{ref}

\Image[paper-4-sigmatropic-map.png]{title=Atom maps for the allylic ketene-acetal and ammonium-ylide rearrangements}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a concrete choice, draw the intermediate in the planar ring orientation used in the question and put the allylic OH on the upper face, with a solid-wedge C-OH bond. Fix either configuration at the R-bearing and methoxy-bearing centres and retain it throughout; no absolute configuration is specified by the question.

Draw the six-membered rearrangement array with its allylic C-C-C segment along the same ring edge and the O-ketene-acetal tether above that edge. Suprafacial transfer forms the new ring-carbon–CH$_2$CO$_2$Et bond on that upper face: draw it as a solid wedge, replacing the original allylic OH with the shifted double bond. The R-bearing and methoxy-bearing <stereocentres> retain their configurations. The opposite OH epimer transfers the tether from the opposite face and gives the opposite new <stereocentre>. Favor the chair-like transition structure that puts the larger tether substituents outward. This explicitly correlates the selected OH face with the new bond rather than invoking a freely rotating allylic <carbocation>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Propionic acid is a weak, relatively high-boiling acid: its normal boiling point is about $141^\circ\mathrm C$, compared with about $118^\circ\mathrm C$ for acetic acid. It provides mild proton catalysis while remaining in the hot orthoester mixture more effectively. This is useful because ketene-acetal formation and the thermal <Johnson-Claisen rearrangement> require heating, whereas strong acid would threaten the pyran <acetal>.

This is a practical catalyst choice, not an absolute mechanistic prohibition on acetic acid; acetic-acid-catalysed orthoester rearrangements are also possible.

= b
{parent=55}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write Im for an imidazolyl group. The acid protonates one imidazole nitrogen, and its carboxylate attacks the central <carbonyl> of <carbonyldiimidazole>. Collapse of the <tetrahedral intermediate> expels imidazole and gives the mixed anhydride $\mathrm{RCO-O-CO-Im}$. Acyl transfer to imidazole, with decarboxylation, produces the activated acyl imidazole $\mathrm{RCO-Im}$, CO$_2$ and imidazole.

The amine attacks the **RCO <carbonyl>** of that acyl imidazole. Its <tetrahedral intermediate> collapses with loss of imidazole; proton transfer gives $\mathrm{RCONHR'}$. The <carbonyl> originally belonging to CDI leaves as CO$_2$, while the product amide <carbonyl> is the original acid <carbonyl>. Imidazole serves both as a leaving group and a proton-transfer base.

\Image[paper-4-carbonyl-routes.png]{title=Activated acyl transfer, hemiaminal opening and carbene formation}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Carbonyldiimidazole> activates the acid under mild conditions without first isolating an acid chloride. CO$_2$ and imidazole are convenient byproducts; the latter also buffers proton transfers. Avoiding strongly acidic acid-chloride-forming conditions can preserve sensitive protecting groups and reduce unwanted side reactions. The activated intermediate still reacts readily with an amine, whereas the unactivated acid and amine would mainly form an ammonium carboxylate.

= c
{parent=55}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The alcohol first attacks CDI, forming an activated O-carbonylimidazole derivative. Its departure gives the resonance-stabilized propargyl/allenyl cation
$$
\mathrm{Ar-C^+(CF_3)-C{\equiv}C-Ph
\leftrightarrow Ar-C(CF_3)=C=C^+(Ph)}.
$$
The ortho amino nitrogen attacks the **central carbon of the allenyl system**, closing the five-membered indole ring. Electron redistribution and loss of the N-H proton establish the indole aromatic system while leaving a <carbene> at the original distal, phenyl-bearing alkyne carbon.

For atom tracking, the original carbinol carbon becomes indole C3, the proximal alkyne carbon becomes C2, and the distal $\mathrm{C(Ph)}$ becomes the exocyclic <carbene>. Attack at the distal alkyne carbon would make a six-membered ring and cannot give the displayed skeleton. This carbene-based rearrangement has a primary experimental precedent.<Rapid assembly of substituted dihydrocycloheptapyrroloindoles by Lisa F. Frey and colleagues>{ref}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With an N-trityl substituent, the <carbene> attacks a nearby trityl phenyl ring. Addition across an arene bond gives a norcaradiene-like cyclopropane, followed by electrocyclic opening to the seven-membered cycloheptatriene: this is a <Buchner ring expansion>. Intramolecular trapping explains the fused product rather than an intermolecular <carbene> reaction.

With N-tert-butyl, there is no pendant arene. Instead the <carbene> inserts into a C-H bond of one tert-butyl methyl group. The new bond between the <carbene> carbon and that methyl carbon closes the five-membered N-containing ring; H is transferred to the <carbene> carbon in the same insertion. The remaining two methyl groups stay on the carbon attached to N.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

No stereochemical information need survive from the starting carbinol. Ionization converts that tetrahedral <stereocentre> into a planar, delocalized propargyl/allenyl cation before cyclization. In achiral conditions the two enantiomeric pathways to the new <stereocentre> in the tert-butyl-derived product are equivalent, so an enantiopure alcohol is expected to give racemic product. A claim of retained enantiopurity would require evidence for unusually effective conformational memory or ion-pair control.

= 56
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=56}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Osmium tetroxide> adds to the alkene through a concerted cyclic addition, giving a five-membered osmate <ester> with both C-O bonds on the same face. Hydrolysis releases the vicinal diol; NMO reoxidizes reduced osmium so that OsO$_4$ can be catalytic.

The two new hydroxyl groups are therefore **syn**. For the drawn substrate, approach opposite the two existing solid-wedge substituents is less crowded: draw **both new C-OH bonds dashed**, while retaining the solid-wedge CO$_2$Bn and NHBoc bonds. The major diol is cis at its two new centres and anti to the pre-existing substituted face.

Syn delivery is the <stereospecificity> of cyclic osmylation; preference for one of the two diastereotopic alkene faces is substrate-controlled <diastereoselectivity>. Attack from the other face would still be syn but would give the minor diastereomer.

= b
{parent=56}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The hydroxylactam is a cyclic <hemiaminal>. Its alkoxide can open to the corresponding <aldehyde>/amidate, $\mathrm{MeNHCO-CH(OBn)-CH_2-CHO}$ in its protonated representation. This exposes an <aldehyde> without reducing or replacing the lactam <carbonyl>.

The ketophosphonate anion attacks that <aldehyde>. Formation and collapse of the phosphorus-containing intermediate give the predominantly E enone $\mathrm{MeNHCO-CH(OBn)-CH_2-CH=CH-COCH_3}$ and phosphate by <Horner-Wadsworth-Emmons reaction>. The amide nitrogen then closes by intramolecular <aza-Michael addition> at the enone beta carbon. <Protonation> of the <ketone> enolate gives the observed five-membered lactam with a $\mathrm{-CH_2COCH_3}$ substituent.

A folded, relatively unencumbered closure places OBn and the developing side chain in outward-facing positions; the expected major product has **OBn and the C5 acetonyl substituent cis**. With the OBn wedge retained, show the new C5–CH$_2$COCH$_3$ bond as a solid wedge. The closure is diastereoselective, while the original wavy <hemiaminal> centre is erased by <aldehyde> formation. This facial prediction rests on the preferred folded conformer rather than retention of either starting <hemiaminal> epimer.

= c
{parent=56}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

This is a thermal <Claisen rearrangement> followed by intramolecular <acetal> exchange. Identify the six-atom array as the cyclopentenone enol-ether C=C–O–CH$_2$–C=C unit. In its concerted $[3,3]$ rearrangement:

* The O–CH$_2$ bond breaks.
* A new C-C bond joins the other end of the cyclopentenone enol double bond to the cyclohexene carbon bearing CH(OEt)$_2$.
* The former ether-bearing cyclopentene carbon becomes a <ketone>, and the cyclohexene double bond becomes the exocyclic methylene.

The intermediate is thus a substituted cyclopentane-1,3-dione, with the <acetal> tether now correctly placed for six-membered-ring closure. Its enol oxygen attacks the <acetal> carbon, replacing OEt and releasing ethanol. Enolization gives the conjugated cyclic enol ether drawn in the product; the second ethoxy group remains.

A chair-like <Claisen rearrangement> transition structure with the phenyl-bearing part of the cyclohexane directed outward avoids severe axial crowding. Suprafacial bond formation and the pre-existing configuration give the two indicated ring-junction hydrogens on opposite faces: the left H is a wedge and the right H a dash in the supplied orientation. Closure favors the <acetal> epimer with outward OEt, drawn as a wedge, while the original Ph remains dashed. This is substrate-controlled diastereoselection followed by conformationally selective <acetal> closure, rather than independent arbitrary assignment of three centres.

= d
{parent=56}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

First the tertiary amine attacks the primary allylic bromide carbon by <SN2 reaction>, giving the quaternary ammonium salt. The less hindered nitrogen face is favored by the pre-existing fused-ring stereochemistry.

DBU removes the proton at the carbon alpha to both the ammonium nitrogen and the <ester> <carbonyl>. The resulting ammonium <ylide> undergoes a concerted <2,3-sigmatropic rearrangement>: the N–CH$_2$(allyl) bond breaks, a bond forms between the <ylide> carbon and the **aryl-bearing terminal allyl carbon**, and the double bond moves to give $\mathrm{-CH(Ar)-C(Me)=CH_2}$. Nitrogen becomes neutral again. An ordinary alkyl substitution would attach the wrong end of the allyl unit.

An envelope-like five-membered transition structure keeps Ar outside the crowded bicyclic framework. With the pre-existing OMe dash and ring-junction geometry fixed, place the newly installed side chain on the solid-wedge face at the former <ylide> carbon and choose the benzylic configuration that places Ar outward, as in the displayed intermediate. This is diastereoselective, suprafacial allyl transfer.

Finally methanesulfonic acid protonates the methyl <acetal>. Methanol leaves to give an oxocarbenium/iminium-stabilized electrophilic centre, which is attacked intramolecularly by the electron-rich arene at its available ortho position. <Deprotonation> restores aromaticity and closes the bridge. The terminal isopropenyl group survives; it is **not** the bond consumed in this final <intramolecular Friedel-Crafts reaction>. The constrained tether selects the displayed bridged diastereomer.

= 57
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=57}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The useful feature is the selenium atom's wavelength-dependent <anomalous X-ray scattering>. Close to its absorption edge the atomic scattering factor is complex:
$$
f_{\rm Se}=f_0+f'(\lambda)+if''(\lambda),\qquad
F(\mathbf h,\lambda)=\sum_j f_j(\lambda)e^{2\pi i\mathbf h\cdot\mathbf r_j}.
$$
Replacing a sulfur-containing side chain by its selenium analogue introduces relatively strong, spatially localized anomalous scatterers. This is the diffraction-physics reason for the label; no biological preparation procedure is needed for the calculation.

The Se substructure can be located from intensity differences or <Patterson maps>. Measurements at several wavelengths change its known complex contribution while leaving most other atoms' scattering nearly unchanged. Interference between that contribution and the unknown remainder constrains the otherwise missing <structure factor> phases. Both wavelength-dependent dispersive differences and differences between <Friedel pairs> contribute to <multiwavelength anomalous diffraction>. Several wavelength constraints resolve phase ambiguities that remain with one intensity difference. The resulting phased Fourier synthesis yields the electron-density map.

= b
{parent=57}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Patterson maps> give interatomic displacement vectors rather than absolute positions. The three strong half-cell vectors are consistent with a face-centered four-point Se substructure. One permissible coordinate set is
$$
(0,0,0),\quad(0,\tfrac12,\tfrac12),\quad
(\tfrac12,0,\tfrac12),\quad(\tfrac12,\tfrac12,0).
$$
Each nonzero vector occurs four times among the twelve ordered Se-Se pairs, explaining equal weights. These positions form a valid four-site orbit of the specified <space group> in a suitable origin convention.

A common translation of all four sites is invisible to a <Patterson map>; absolute coordinates are therefore not uniquely fixed by these three peaks. Subsequent phases are stated in the origin convention above. The remaining atoms need not have face-centered translational symmetry merely because the dominant Se substructure does.

= c
{parent=57}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For reflection $222$, the positional phase factor is $e^{2\pi i(2x+2y+2z)}=1$ for each of the four sites just found. Treat the supplied anomalous increment as the imaginary term $if''$, with $f''=4.8$ electrons per Se atom. Then
$$
\Delta F(222)=4i(4.8)=19.2i\ {\rm electrons}.
$$
Its magnitude is **$19.2$ electrons** and its phase is **$+90^\circ$** relative to the real axis in this structure-factor convention. Translating the origin rotates both this reference contribution and the ordinary <structure factor> consistently; the physically relevant phase difference is invariant.

= d
{parent=57}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write the ordinary <structure factor> as $F_0=|F_0|e^{i\phi}$ and use $F=F_0+19.2i$. The intensities imply
$$
|F_0|=\sqrt{13130}=114.59,\qquad
15430=13130+19.2^2+2(19.2)|F_0|\sin\phi.
$$
Consequently $\sin\phi=0.43893$, and the two possible ordinary phases are
$$
\boxed{\phi\simeq26.0^\circ\quad\hbox{or}\quad154.0^\circ.}
$$
The twofold ambiguity is expected from a single anomalous-intensity constraint. The calculation assumes that the specified imaginary increment is the only change requiring inclusion; an additional unspecified real dispersive change could not be reconstructed from these data alone.

= e
{parent=57}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Without significant anomalous scattering, the <electron density> is effectively real and <Friedel's law> gives $F_0(-\mathbf h)=F_0(\mathbf h)^*$, hence $I(\overline{222})=13130$.

With anomalous scattering the atomic imaginary term retains its sign when the reflection index reverses. Here $\Delta F(-222)=19.2i$, so
$$
I(-222)=|F_0^*+19.2i|^2
=13130+19.2^2-2(19.2)|F_0|\sin\phi
=11567.3\ {\rm electrons}^2.
$$
Thus the second expected intensity is approximately **$1.157\times10^4\ {\rm electrons}^2$**. Conjugating the complete anomalous <structure factor> would incorrectly impose Friedel equality on the second experiment.

= 58
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=58}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Reality of the <electron density> implies $F(-h)=F(h)^*$. Writing $F(h)=|F(h)|e^{i\phi_h}$ and pairing positive and negative indices gives
$$
\rho(x)=F(0)+2\sum_{h=1}^\infty |F(h)|
\cos(2\pi hx-\phi_h).
$$
The sign in the cosine follows from the supplied negative-sign Fourier-synthesis convention. $F(0)$ is the mean <electron density> in the fractional-coordinate normalization.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With an origin at an inversion center, the <structure factors> are real, so their phases are either $0$ or $\pi$. Defining $S(h)=\operatorname{sgn}F(h)$ gives
$$
\rho(x)=F(0)+2\sum_{h=1}^\infty S(h)|F(h)|\cos(2\pi hx).
$$
Centrosymmetry converts the phase problem into a sign problem; it does not imply that every <structure factor> is positive.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a strong $h$ term, maxima lie where $S(h)\cos(2\pi hx)=+1$. At either possible set of such maxima, $\cos(4\pi hx)=+1$: doubling a phase of either $0$ or $\pi$ gives zero modulo $2\pi$. A strong $2h$ term reinforces those positive maxima when $S(2h)=+1$. The <direct-methods pair relationship> is therefore
$$
S(h)^2S(2h)\simeq+1,\qquad\hbox{hence }S(2h)\simeq+1.
$$
A negative strong second harmonic would oppose the maxima favored by the first and tend to create implausible negative or flattened density elsewhere. The relationship is a strong-reflection inference, not a sign identity for arbitrary weak coefficients.

\Image[paper-4-phase-signs.png]{title=How the sign of a second harmonic changes reinforcement of an atomic maximum}

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The inference is most useful when the contributing normalized amplitudes are large. Positivity gives a precise illustration: let $f_h=F(h)/F(0)$ and regard $\rho(x)/F(0)$ as a probability density over a period. From $\langle\cos^2(2\pi hx)\rangle\geq\langle\cos(2\pi hx)\rangle^2$,
$$
f_{2h}\geq2f_h^2-1.
$$
Thus $|f_h|>1/\sqrt2$ forces $F(2h)>0$. If $F(h)$ is weak, this bound allows either sign; a small $F(2h)$ also contributes too little to the density sketch to impose a persuasive sign constraint. Large unnormalized intensity alone should not be confused with a large amplitude relative to $F(0)$.

= b
{parent=58}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The dominant contributions are $2F(10)\cos(2\pi x)+2F(01)\cos(2\pi y)$. Their extrema occur at $x=0$ or $\tfrac12$ and independently $y=0$ or $\tfrac12$, modulo unit translations. The four candidate positions are
$$
(0,0),\quad(\tfrac12,0),\quad(0,\tfrac12),\quad
(\tfrac12,\tfrac12).
$$
Which is a maximum rather than a minimum or saddle depends on the two signs. Higher Fourier terms sharpen these broad maxima into atomic peaks.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The sign choices that make each dominant cosine positive at the selected maximum are:

* $(0,0)$: $S(10)=+$ and $S(01)=+$.
* $(\tfrac12,0)$: $S(10)=-$ and $S(01)=+$.
* $(0,\tfrac12)$: $S(10)=+$ and $S(01)=-$.
* $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$: $S(10)=-$ and $S(01)=-$.

Equivalently, at a selected maximum $(x_0,y_0)$, the signs are $(-1)^{2x_0}$ and $(-1)^{2y_0}$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The third term is $2F(11)\cos[2\pi(x+y)]$. To reinforce a maximum at $(x_0,y_0)$ from the preceding table, its sign should be
$$
S(11)=(-1)^{2x_0+2y_0}=S(10)S(01).
$$
Hence the <direct-methods triplet relationship> is $S(10)S(01)S(11)\simeq+1$ for the strong reflections under consideration. This maximizes constructive density at shared atomic positions. For weak or competing reflections the relation is probabilistic rather than an unconditional algebraic theorem.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Moving the origin by $\mathbf t$ changes a structure-factor phase by $-2\pi\mathbf h\cdot\mathbf t$ in the corresponding coordinate convention. The triplet phase
$$
\phi_{\mathbf h}+\phi_{\mathbf k}-\phi_{\mathbf h+\mathbf k}
$$
is unchanged because the three origin shifts cancel. In a centrosymmetric origin convention, the associated sign product $S(\mathbf h)S(\mathbf k)S(\mathbf h+\mathbf k)$ is therefore invariant.

For example shifting $x$ by half a period flips both $S(10)$ and $S(11)$, leaving $S(01)$ unchanged; shifting $y$ flips the other corresponding pair. A general origin may make the factors complex, but the phase-invariant statement remains valid.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Set $\mathbf k=\mathbf h$ in the <direct-methods triplet relationship>. It becomes $S(\mathbf h)^2S(2\mathbf h)\simeq+1$, and since the square of a nonzero sign is one, $S(2\mathbf h)\simeq+1$. This recovers the <direct-methods pair relationship>, with the same strong-amplitude qualifications.

= 59
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=59}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $H^{(0)}|n\rangle=E_n^{(0)}|n\rangle$ with a nondegenerate reference <eigenvalue>, and choose intermediate normalization $\langle n|n^{(1)}\rangle=0$. The first-order equation is
$$
(H^{(0)}-E_n^{(0)})|n^{(1)}\rangle
=-(H^{(1)}-E_n^{(1)})|n\rangle.
$$
Projection on $|j\rangle$, $j\ne n$, gives $\langle j|n^{(1)}\rangle=H_{jn}^{(1)}/(E_n^{(0)}-E_j^{(0)})$. The second-order projected equation gives $E_n^{(2)}=\langle n|H^{(1)}|n^{(1)}\rangle$, hence
$$
E_n^{(2)}
=-\sum_{j\ne n}
\frac{|H_{nj}^{(1)}|^2}{E_j^{(0)}-E_n^{(0)}}.
$$
The sum runs over a complete orthonormal reference basis except the reference state; continuum states require integration if present. Degenerate states with zero denominators require <degenerate perturbation theory> first. These are expansion coefficients in $E_n=E_n^{(0)}+\lambda E_n^{(1)}+\lambda^2E_n^{(2)}+\cdots$.

= b
{parent=59}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The electric potential is $\varepsilon(x-a/2)$, so inside the box
$$
H=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{d^2}{dx^2}
+q\varepsilon(x-a/2),\quad
H^{(0)}=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{d^2}{dx^2},\quad
H^{(1)}=x-a/2,\quad \lambda=q\varepsilon.
$$
The infinite walls impose zero <wavefunction> at $0,a$. The normalized <particle in a box> states are $\psi_n(x)=\sqrt{2/a}\sin(n\pi x/a)$ and $E_n^{(0)}=n^2h^2/(8ma^2)$ for positive integers $n$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The probability density $|\psi_n(x)|^2$ is symmetric about $a/2$, while $x-a/2$ is antisymmetric. Therefore
$$
E_n^{(1)}=\int_0^a|\psi_n(x)|^2(x-a/2)\,dx=0.
$$
The use of a midpoint-zero electric potential matters: adding a constant potential would add the corresponding uniform energy shift.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Reflection $x\mapsto a-x$ preserves the walls and kinetic operator but sends $\lambda(x-a/2)$ to $-\lambda(x-a/2)$. The Hamiltonians at $\lambda$ and $-\lambda$ are unitarily equivalent, so each analytic nondegenerate energy branch obeys $E_n(\lambda)=E_n(-\lambda)$. Its Taylor expansion contains only even powers: all odd-order energy coefficients vanish, not merely the first-order expectation.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The midpoint-odd perturbation couples the <ground state> only to even-index states. Direct integration gives
$$
\langle1|x-a/2|2j\rangle
=-\frac{16aj}{\pi^2(4j^2-1)^2},\qquad
E_{2j}^{(0)}-E_1^{(0)}
=\frac{h^2}{8ma^2}(4j^2-1).
$$
Insertion in the second-order expression yields
$$
E_1^{(2)}=
-\frac{2048ma^4}{h^2\pi^4}
\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{j^2}{(4j^2-1)^5}.
$$
It is negative because all coupled states lie above the <ground state>. This is the coefficient of $\lambda^2$; the physical energy shift includes the additional factor $q^2\varepsilon^2$.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

With the supplied sum, the second-order approximation is
$$
E_1\simeq\frac{h^2}{8ma^2}
-\frac{2048ma^4q^2\varepsilon^2}{h^2\pi^4}
(4.12\times10^{-3}).
$$
For an electron in the $1\,\mu\mathrm m$ box, $E_1^{(0)}=6.02\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm J$ and the correction is $-4.61\times10^{-27}\,\mathrm J$, giving **$5.56\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm J=3.47\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{eV}$**.

For $10\,\mu\mathrm m$, the formal result is approximately **$-4.61\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm J=-2.88\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{eV}$**. It is not a credible approximation: even the minimum electric potential energy in that box is only $-5\,\mu\mathrm{eV}$, whereas this answer is much lower. The ground-to-first-excited-state mixing estimate increases from $0.160$ to $160$ when the length grows tenfold. The shift grows as $a^4$ while the level spacing decreases as $a^{-2}$, so the larger box is far outside the perturbative regime.

= c
{parent=59}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The first excited unperturbed level is twofold degenerate, spanned by $|1,2\rangle$ and $|2,1\rangle$, with energy $5h^2/(8ma^2)$. The perturbation is $q\varepsilon_Q(x-a/2)(y-a/2)$. Both diagonal matrix elements vanish, but the off-diagonal element does not:
$$
W_{12,21}=q\varepsilon_Q
\left[-\frac{16a}{9\pi^2}\right]^2
=\frac{256q\varepsilon_Qa^2}{81\pi^4}.
$$
Diagonalizing the $2\times2$ matrix with zero diagonal gives first-order shifts
$$
\boxed{\Delta E^{(1)}_\pm=
\pm\frac{256q\varepsilon_Qa^2}{81\pi^4}},
$$
with symmetric/antisymmetric combinations $(|1,2\rangle\pm|2,1\rangle)/\sqrt2$. Their order depends on the sign of $q\varepsilon_Q$; the lower branch has shift $-|W_{12,21}|$. Applying nondegenerate <perturbation theory> to either product state would miss the splitting.

= 60
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=60}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Define $h_{pq}=\langle p|\hat h|q\rangle$, the one-electron kinetic plus nuclear-attraction integral, and physicists' two-electron integrals
$$
\langle pq|rs\rangle=\iint\phi_p(1)\phi_q(2)
\frac1{r_{12}}\phi_r(1)\phi_s(2)\,d1\,d2
$$
in <atomic units>. With $E_{\rm NN}$ the zero-electron nuclear-repulsion term, the <Slater determinant> expectation is
$$
E_0=E_{\rm NN}+\sum_i h_{ii}
+\frac12\sum_{ij}\bigl(\langle ij|ij\rangle-\langle ij|ji\rangle\bigr).
$$
The occupied sums include spin-orbitals, so the exchange integral vanishes for incompatible spins without needing a separate closed-shell factor.

= b
{parent=60}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <Hartree-Fock wavefunction> is stationary in energy with respect to all infinitesimal occupied-virtual orbital rotations, subject to orbital orthonormality. The determinant then satisfies the self-consistent Hartree-Fock equations. Stationarity alone does not guarantee that it is the lowest-energy or even a locally stable determinant.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

$\Phi_i^a$ is the normalized determinant obtained by replacing occupied spin-orbital $i$ in $\Phi_0$ with previously unoccupied orbital $a$, using a consistent antisymmetric determinant ordering. Thus $i=1,\ldots,N$ labels occupied orbitals and $a=N+1,\ldots,2M$ labels virtual orbitals. Occupied-to-occupied rotations only change the representation of the same occupied subspace; the occupied-virtual rotations describe distinct nearby determinants.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Orthogonality of the determinants gives $\langle\Phi^{(1)}|\Phi^{(1)}\rangle=1+O(\lambda^2)$. To first order the normalized energy is therefore
$$
E^{(1)}=E_0+2\sum_{ia}\lambda_i^a
\langle\Phi_0|\hat H|\Phi_i^a\rangle+O(\lambda^2).
$$
<Slater-Condon rules> give the matrix element as the occupied-virtual Fock element
$$
F_{ia}=h_{ia}+\sum_{j\ {\rm occ}}
\left(\langle ij|aj\rangle-\langle ij|ja\rangle\right).
$$
Stationarity for independently variable real $\lambda_i^a$ requires every $F_{ia}=0$. This is <Brillouin's theorem>. Because $F$ is Hermitian, occupied and virtual blocks can separately be diagonalized; canonical orbitals then satisfy $\hat F\phi_p=\gamma_p\phi_p$.

= c
{parent=60}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The stationary determinant should be stable against orbital rotations: its orbital-energy Hessian must have no negative direction. A suitable local minimum, preferably the lowest accessible determinant of the appropriate symmetry and spin, can approximate the <ground state>; a stationary excited determinant or saddle generally cannot. Stability is still not a guarantee of small electron-correlation error.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

An example is the doubly occupied antibonding configuration $(\sigma_u)^2$, rather than the bonding $(\sigma_g)^2$ configuration dominant near equilibrium. It can be a self-consistent stationary determinant because symmetry makes the corresponding occupied-virtual Fock mixing vanish, while its bonding energy and nodal structure are inappropriate for the <ground state>. Solving the Hartree-Fock stationarity equations does not impose ground-state occupancy automatically.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The reference, singly excited and doubly excited determinants are mutually orthogonal. Singles have amplitude $O(\lambda)$ and doubles $O(\lambda^2)$, so only the reference norm and squares of single-excitation amplitudes contribute through second order:
$$
\langle\Phi^{(2)}|\Phi^{(2)}\rangle
=1+\sum_{ia}(\lambda_i^a)^2+O(\lambda^4).
$$
There is no reference-double cross term. The stated expansion has no cubic contribution because its single and double sectors are orthogonal.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

At a Hartree-Fock stationary point the reference-single terms vanish. Expanding the normalized expectation through second order gives
$$
\mathcal E^{(2)}
=\sum_{ia,jb}\lambda_i^a\lambda_j^b
\left[
\langle\Phi_i^a|(\hat H-E_0)|\Phi_j^b\rangle
+\langle\Phi_0|\hat H|\Phi_{ij}^{ab}\rangle
\right].
$$
The reference-double term arises twice in the numerator and cancels the factor $1/2$ in the <wavefunction>. The normalization subtracts $E_0\sum_{ia}(\lambda_i^a)^2$.

Using <Slater-Condon rules> in canonical orbitals,
$$
\langle\Phi_i^a|(\hat H-E_0)|\Phi_j^b\rangle
=(\gamma_a-\gamma_i)\delta_{ij}\delta_{ab}
+\langle aj|ib\rangle-\langle aj|bi\rangle,
$$
and $\langle\Phi_0|\hat H|\Phi_{ij}^{ab}\rangle
=\langle ab|ij\rangle-\langle ab|ji\rangle$. Combining them gives the requested $X_{ij}^{ab}$. The orbital energies are
$$
\gamma_p=h_{pp}+
\sum_{k\ {\rm occ}}\left(\langle pk|pk\rangle-\langle pk|kp\rangle\right),
$$
for either occupied or virtual $p$. Diagonalizing the occupied and virtual Fock blocks is the suitable orbital choice that makes the delta form valid for coincident as well as distinct indices.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The real symmetric quadratic form is positive for every nonzero vector of occupied-virtual rotation parameters precisely when its matrix $X$ is positive definite. Then the Hartree-Fock determinant is a strict local minimum within the considered real orbital-rotation space. A negative <eigenvalue> gives an unstable lowering direction; a zero <eigenvalue> requires higher-order analysis. Stability in this space does not by itself prove global minimality or stability under more general spin/complex-orbital variations.

= 61
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=61}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For fixed electron-electron interaction and electron number, a nondegenerate ground-state <electron density> determines the external potential up to an additive constant, and hence determines the Hamiltonian and ground-state observables.

Suppose two potentials $v,v'$ differing by more than a constant have different <ground states> $\Psi,\Psi'$ but the same density $\rho$. Strict <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle> inequalities give
$$
E< E'+\int[v(\mathbf r)-v'(\mathbf r)]\rho(\mathbf r)\,d^3r,
$$
$$
E'< E+\int[v'(\mathbf r)-v(\mathbf r)]\rho(\mathbf r)\,d^3r.
$$
Adding produces the contradiction $E+E'<E+E'$. This proves the first <Hohenberg-Kohn theorem> under the stated nondegeneracy assumptions. A constant potential shift changes energy by $N$ times that constant without changing the density.<Inhomogeneous electron gas by Pierre Hohenberg and Walter Kohn>[Density-potential uniqueness argument]{ref}

= b
{parent=61}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Far from a neutral bound system, the slowest-decaying electron-removal amplitude is controlled by the first ionization energy $I=E_{N-1}-E_N>0$. Its asymptotic <wavefunction> satisfies a free-particle-like equation at negative energy $-I$, giving a decay exponent $\kappa=\sqrt{2m_eI}/\hbar$. Density is quadratic in the amplitude, so
$$
\boxed{a=2\kappa=\frac{2\sqrt{2m_eI}}{\hbar}}
$$
or $a=2\sqrt{2I}$ in <atomic units>. Power-law radial factors and angular dependence do not alter this leading exponential coefficient. It is the ionization energy, not the electron affinity, that controls this tail.

= c
{parent=61}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Wigner-Seitz radius> $r_s$ is the radius of a sphere containing one electron at the local uniform density:
$$
\frac43\pi r_s^3\rho=1,\qquad
r_s=\left(\frac3{4\pi\rho}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
In <atomic units>, the unpolarized homogeneous electron gas has exchange energy per electron $-0.4582/r_s$. The <local density approximation> applies that uniform-gas result at each position using the local density, then integrates the result weighted by $\rho$. It assumes local uniform-gas behavior rather than resolving nonlocal exchange explicitly.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Substitution gives
$$
\varepsilon_X^{\rm LDA}(\mathbf r)
=-0.4582\left(\frac{4\pi}3\right)^{1/3}\rho(\mathbf r)^{1/3}
\simeq-0.7386\,\rho(\mathbf r)^{1/3}.
$$
For $\rho\propto e^{-ar}$, this decays as $-C e^{-ar/3}$, with possible subleading radial prefactors. It therefore vanishes exponentially rather than with the required algebraic $-c/r$ exchange-energy tail.

= d
{parent=61}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

$x_\sigma=|\nabla\rho_\sigma|/\rho_\sigma^{4/3}$ is a dimensionless reduced density gradient. It measures inhomogeneity relative to the local density scale, vanishes for a uniform gas, and allows a <generalized gradient approximation> correction to distinguish rapidly varying density from slowly varying density with the same local value.

The parameter $\beta$ controls the correction strength in the <Becke 1988 exchange functional>; it was fitted to atomic exchange energies. The large-gradient denominator simultaneously produces the intended asymptotic behavior, so its role is more constrained than an arbitrary multiplicative adjustment.<Density-functional exchange-energy approximation with correct asymptotic behavior by Axel D. Becke>[Reduced-gradient form, fitted parameter and asymptotic construction]{ref}

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For typical atoms and molecules, the <Becke 1988 exchange functional> improves exchange energies and commonly improves molecular energetics relative to exchange-only <local density approximation>, because it accounts for density gradients and corrects important nonuniformity errors. This is a general trend, not a theorem for every observable or system.

A comparison of total molecular predictions also depends on the correlation functional, basis, spin treatment and geometry. B88 is an exchange approximation, not by itself a complete specification of an exchange-correlation calculation.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a tail $\rho_\sigma\sim A_\sigma e^{-ar}$,
$$
|\nabla\rho_\sigma|\sim a\rho_\sigma,\qquad
x_\sigma\sim a\rho_\sigma^{-1/3},\qquad
\sinh^{-1}x_\sigma\sim ar/3.
$$
At large $r$, the B88 correction integrand becomes
$$
-\rho_\sigma^{4/3}
\frac{\beta x_\sigma^2}{6\beta x_\sigma\sinh^{-1}x_\sigma}
\sim-\frac{a\rho_\sigma}{6(ar/3)}
=-\frac{\rho_\sigma}{2r}.
$$
Summing the spin densities and dividing by total density gives
$$
\varepsilon_X^{\rm B88}\sim-\frac1{2r},\qquad
\boxed{c=\tfrac12}
$$
in <atomic units>. The exponentially decaying LDA term is negligible in this limit. The parameter $\beta$ cancels from the leading asymptotic term.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

In the usual exchange-hole representation, the hole around an electron integrates to charge deficit $-1$. Far outside a finite system, the localized hole therefore looks like a unit opposite charge at distance $r$. The Coulomb interaction has leading scale $-1/r$, and the exchange pair energy carries the usual factor $1/2$ to avoid double counting, giving exchange energy per electron $-1/(2r)$.

This energy-per-electron tail should not be confused with the exchange or exchange-correlation potential, whose leading coefficient is generally $-1/r$ in the corresponding exact finite-system setting. Local energy-density representations also require a specified gauge; the question uses the conventional exchange-hole interpretation.

= 62
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=62}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

