= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Langmuir adsorption model> assumes equivalent independent sites, a maximum of one adsorbate per site, no lateral adsorbate interactions, reversible adsorption-desorption equilibrium and non-dissociative <adsorption> here. Surface coverage obeys $\theta=bC/(1+bC)$ for the solution analogue.

<BET adsorption> allows multiple layers: occupation of one layer creates sites for further <adsorption> above it, and higher-layer binding is treated differently from first-layer binding, commonly using a condensation-like energy. Langmuir saturation is monolayer saturation; the BET model is not simply Langmuir with a different numerical equilibrium constant.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Invert the stated expression:
$$
\frac1\Gamma=\frac{1+bC}{\Gamma_\infty bC}
=\frac{1}{b\Gamma_\infty}\frac1C+\frac1{\Gamma_\infty}.
$$
Hence the line of $1/\Gamma$ against $1/C$ has slope $M=1/(b\Gamma_\infty)$ and intercept $1/\Gamma_\infty$. Their ratio gives $b=(\mathrm{intercept})/(\mathrm{slope})$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

A least-squares straight-line fit in the requested reciprocal coordinates gives approximately
$$
\frac1\Gamma=0.00989\,\frac1C+0.01197
$$
when the supplied numerical units are used. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_\infty\simeq83.5\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-2}}},
\qquad
\boxed{b\simeq1.21\,\mathrm{m^3\,mmol^{-1}}}.
$$
The slope and intercept units follow from the plotted reciprocal quantities. These parameters are the result of the requested reciprocal linear fit; fitting the original nonlinear isotherm with a different error model would give slightly different estimates.

\Image[paper-4-adsorption-fit.png]{title=Requested reciprocal fit and its reconstructed adsorption isotherm}

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The inferred saturation density is physically implausible for a single molecular layer in the printed units:
$$
A_{\rm molecule}=\frac1{(83.5\times10^{-3})N_A}
\simeq2.0\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{m^2}
=2.0\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{nm^2}.
$$
That is far smaller than an atomic cross-section, let alone a butyric-acid molecule. The near-linear reciprocal plot therefore does not validate the physical monolayer interpretation. Possible issues include reported units or normalization, inaccessible/internal area not accounted for correctly, multilayer <adsorption> or other uptake processes. The numbers must not silently be relabelled as micromoles to obtain a more comfortable molecular area.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Electric-dipole <sum-frequency generation spectroscopy> is a coherent second-order optical probe. It is sensitive to appropriately oriented, symmetry-allowed molecular responses at the interface; randomly oriented populations can cancel in the orientational average, and centrosymmetric arrangements give no net electric-dipole second-order response.

Thus an ordered fraction of surface-bound carboxylate or hydrocarbon groups may dominate the measured spectrum, while disordered, differently oriented or weakly active adsorbates contribute little. The observed vibrational intensity is not a direct count of every molecule retained in an <adsorption> measurement. Selection rules, polarization and interference between contributions also matter.

