= Solution
{parent=i}

For competing reactant and product species on shared equivalent sites, add each $b_jC_j$ term to the denominator:
$$
\theta_i=\frac{b_iC_i}{1+\sum_jb_jC_j}.
$$
Rapid product <desorption> makes product coverages small, reducing competitive site blocking and simplifying the rate to reactant coverage alone. Without that assumption, product <adsorption> can inhibit conversion and make the apparent rate evolve as products accumulate.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

$0.1\,\mathrm M=100\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}=10^5\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-3}}$. Therefore
$$
\theta_p=\frac{0.36(10^5)}{1+0.36(10^5)}
=0.999972,
\qquad
v=2.1\theta_p\simeq
\boxed{2.10\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-3}s^{-1}}}.
$$
The surface is almost saturated, so the rate approaches the supplied limiting constant. This assumes rapid <adsorption> equilibrium, negligible product coverage, no competing species and unchanged substrate area/photochemical conditions.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For simultaneous competitive <adsorption>, use the butyric-acid constant inferred in part (c), $b_b\simeq1.21\,\mathrm{m^3\,mmol^{-1}}$, and $C_b=10^6\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-3}}$:
$$
\theta_p=\frac{0.36(10^5)}
{1+0.36(10^5)+1.21(10^6)}
\simeq0.0289.
$$
The propionic-acid rate is therefore approximately
$$
\boxed{v_p=2.1\theta_p\simeq0.061\,\mathrm{mmol\,m^{-3}s^{-1}}}.
$$
The abundant, more strongly adsorbing butyric acid occupies most common sites, reducing propionic conversion by roughly a factor of $35$. This calculation follows the requested competitive Langmuir model despite the physical concern about the fitted saturation density noted earlier.

