= Solution
{parent=i}

For the ordinary <esters>, <hydroxide> adds to the acyl carbon, producing a tetrahedral oxyanion; collapse expels 2,4-dinitrophenoxide, followed by acid-base equilibration to the benzoate. This is base-promoted acyl-oxygen cleavage, a <BAC2 mechanism>. Rate-limiting addition naturally gives the observed bimolecular dependence on <ester> and OH$^-$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Hammett equation> is $\log(k_X/k_H)=\rho\sigma_X$. The positive slope, $\rho=+1.97$, means electron-withdrawing para substituents accelerate hydrolysis. They increase <carbonyl> electrophilicity and stabilize the additional negative charge associated with the addition transition state/<tetrahedral intermediate>. Strong electron donation has the opposite effect. <Hydroxide> addition to the acyl carbon, followed by tetrahedral-intermediate collapse and aryloxide loss, is consistent with both this trend and the bimolecular rate law.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The neutral para-OH <ester> B lies **above** the ordinary Hammett line, so an additional accelerating pathway is needed; ordinary resonance donation by OH alone predicts the wrong direction.

The phenoxide B$^-$ lies far **below** the line: its strong resonance donation reduces acyl electrophilicity, and an anionic <ester> is less favorable for approach by OH$^-$. A single neutral-substituent $\sigma$ parameter is also a poor description of this charged, strongly conjugated substrate. These deviations should not be forced onto the same straight line.

= b
{parent=66}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The kink indicates that **two pathways with different leaving-group sensitivities compete**: one dominates for good leaving groups and another for poorer ones. A single straight-line free-energy relationship is insufficient. The additional hydroxide-order information can be accommodated by rapid phenol <deprotonation>,
$$
\mathrm{C+OH^-\rightleftharpoons C^-+H_2O},\qquad
[\mathrm{C^-}]=K_{\rm OH}[\mathrm C][\mathrm{OH^-}].
$$
Two rates are then possible. A route with one <hydroxide> equivalent in its overall kinetic dependence contributes $k_2[\mathrm C][\mathrm{OH^-}]$. Attack of a **further <hydroxide>** on phenoxide <ester> contributes
$$
k_{\rm add}[\mathrm{C^-}][\mathrm{OH^-}]
=k_{\rm add}K_{\rm OH}[\mathrm C][\mathrm{OH^-}]^2.
$$
Thus $k_3=k_{\rm add}K_{\rm OH}$. The third-order term need not involve a simultaneous collision of three molecules: sequential acid-base equilibration followed by bimolecular attack produces it.

= c
{parent=66}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Hydroxide> attack at the aromatic carbon bearing the leaving-group oxygen, followed by <SNAr reaction>, would put solvent-derived $^{18}$O into the liberated dinitrophenol. Its absence rules out that aryl C-O cleavage route, conventionally called **BAr2**, as the significant pathway.

Acyl C-O cleavage instead releases the original aryloxy oxygen with the leaving group, so unlabeled dinitrophenol is expected for both tetrahedral acyl substitution and the elimination route below.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The three regions represent competing pathways whose leaving-group sensitivities differ.

* **Region 1, $\beta_{\rm lg}=-1.33$:** good leaving groups permit <E1cB mechanism>. OH$^-$ first deprotonates the para phenol. The phenoxide lone pair then pushes through the ring, with loss of ArO$^-$ from the acyl group, giving a <quinoid ketene>, schematically $\mathrm{O=C_6H_4=C=O}$. Water/<hydroxide> traps the ketene and proton transfers restore the aromatic hydroxybenzoate. Rate-limiting fragmentation has extensive C–OAr cleavage, hence strong dependence on leaving-group basicity.
* **Region 2, $\beta_{\rm lg}=-0.35$:** as ArO$^-$ becomes a worse leaving group, elimination slows steeply and ordinary <hydroxide> addition to neutral C becomes competitive/dominant. The tetrahedral acyl-substitution pathway is much less sensitive to leaving-group p$K_a$.
* **Region 3, $\beta_{\rm lg}=-0.20$:** <hydroxide> attacks the deprotonated phenoxide <ester> C$^-$. Its low electrophilicity and anion-anion interaction make addition slow. The leaving group is little involved in that addition transition state. Combined with prior <deprotonation>, this is the third-order term.

The bend in the upper plot is a crossover of mechanisms, not a discontinuous change in the identity of the leaving group. The lower plot is the separate hydroxide-on-phenoxide pathway.

<Model experiments implicate a benzoquinoneketene intermediate in LCP synthesis by Jeffrey M. Robertson and colleagues>{ref}

\Image[paper-4-hydrolysis-routes.png]{title=Parallel acyl-substitution and conjugate-base elimination pathways}

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The positive $\Delta S^\ddagger=+40\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$ is compatible with fragmentation/desolvation in the elimination route. A simple rate-limiting association of <hydroxide> with a neutral <ester> would usually have a less favorable <activation entropy>.

The measured activation parameters belong to the overall second-order rate constant: the acid-base pre-equilibrium and solvent reorganization contribute as well. Consequently positive <activation entropy> supports the fragmentation interpretation together with the leaving-group slope, but does not by itself prove a particular elementary transition structure.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For B, $\Delta V^\ddagger=+12\,\mathrm{cm^3\,mol^{-1}}$ is consistent with bond fission and release/reorganization of electrostricted solvent on reaching a fragmentation transition state. The ordinary methoxy <ester> has $\Delta V^\ddagger=-13\,\mathrm{cm^3\,mol^{-1}}$, consistent with associative <hydroxide> addition and the compact, strongly solvated developing tetrahedral species.

The opposite signs corroborate distinct mechanisms. Since both values include solvation and any pre-equilibria, they should not be interpreted merely as geometric volumes of isolated molecules.

= 67
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}

= a
{parent=67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Protonation> converts OH into a water leaving group; ionization then gives the diarylmethyl cation. The labeled C-H/D bond is retained, so $k_H/k_D=1.18$ is a **normal alpha-secondary <kinetic isotope effect>**, not a primary C-H cleavage effect.

At the labeled carbon, tetrahedral geometry becomes approximately trigonal planar. The associated change in bending vibrations and zero-point energies favors the H isotopologue in a substantially carbocation-like transition state. The two anisyl rings stabilize that cation and make this ionization pathway plausible.

= b
{parent=67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A plausible ionic catalytic cycle is reversible thiocyanate addition to the activated maleic-acid double bond, with proton transfers, followed by rotation about the resulting C-C single bond and elimination that regenerates thiocyanate and forms the less crowded trans alkene.

The retained vinylic labels pass from approximately sp$^2$ toward sp$^3$ at the addition stage. Stiffer bending modes in that direction give an **inverse secondary <kinetic isotope effect>**, consistent with $0.86$. The cycle changes alkene geometry without requiring cleavage of either original labeled C-H bond. Since two positions are labeled, $0.86$ is a combined effect; equal independent contributions would each be $\sqrt{0.86}=0.927$.

The isotope effect supports an addition/elimination route rather than simple rotation of an intact double bond; it does not, alone, identify every proton-transfer order.

= c
{parent=67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The natural mechanism is ionizing solvolysis: C-OTs cleavage gives a cyclopentyl cation/ion pair, capture by acetic acid gives the protonated acetate <ester>, and <deprotonation> gives product.

The **$1.15$** effect from H/D at the leaving-group carbon is normal alpha-secondary: that carbon develops trigonal <carbocation> character. The **$2.06$** effect from four neighboring labels is a combined beta-secondary effect. Adjacent C-H sigma bonds stabilize the developing empty p orbital by <hyperconjugation>; replacing H by D changes the vibrational stabilization and slows ionization. If the four contributions were equal and independent, each would be $2.06^{1/4}=1.198$.

None of those labeled C-H bonds is broken. The isotope effects support substantial <carbocation> character, although distinguishing a free cation from an intimate ion pair needs additional evidence.

\Image[paper-4-isotope-geometry.png]{title=Retained-bond geometry and the sign of secondary isotope effects}

= d
{parent=67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Bridgehead substitution proceeds by ionization and solvent capture; backside displacement at the adamantyl bridgehead is geometrically inaccessible. The rigid cage prevents the labeled bonds from sampling all the favorable alignments available in cyclopentyl solvolysis.

The two labeled bonds in the adjacent methylene group are poorly oriented for ordinary C-H-to-empty-orbital <hyperconjugation>, explaining an effect close to **$1.000$**. Cage C-C sigma donation and geometrical relaxation can still stabilize the developing adamantyl cation. The three remote bridgehead labels perturb that coupled framework and give the smaller normal effect **$1.073$**, or about $1.024$ per label if contributions are equivalent.

This is a geometrical and remote secondary isotope effect. It does not imply cleavage of the remote C-H bonds, and absence of a beta effect at one selected site does not establish absence of a cation.

= e
{parent=67}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Triethylamine attacks the benzyl carbon and bromide leaves, giving the quaternary benzyltriethylammonium salt. A useful substitution transition structure is
$$
\left[\mathrm{Et_3N\cdots C(L)_2(Ar)\cdots Br}\right]^\ddagger,
$$
with developing N-C bonding and breaking C-Br bonding. The C-H/D bonds remain intact, so the small effects are secondary.

For X=NO$_2$, a relatively associative <SN2 reaction> transition state can give a small inverse effect, $0.988$, through altered bending constraints. The unsubstituted case, $0.993$, is close to cancellation of opposing contributions. Electron-donating OMe stabilizes benzylic positive charge, favoring a looser, more dissociative transition state and a slightly normal effect, $1.014$; it also explains the increased relative rate, $7.4$ versus $1$ for H and $0.52$ for NO$_2$.

Thus the data indicate increasing dissociative character with electron donation. A contribution from a separate ionization pathway is possible for the strongest donor, but these small isotope effects and three relative rates do not uniquely require a sharp SN2-to-SN1 mechanism switch.
